    <!-- 编辑判断结论 -->
    <div class="verdict">
      <p class="verdict-title">Our editorial take</p>
      <p class="verdict-text">
        For most buyers under $500, the <strong>TCL QM6K</strong> is the safest pick: it's the
        runner-up in Wirecutter's 2026 budget-TV guide, its specs are verified on TCL's own site
        (144&nbsp;Hz, QD-Mini&nbsp;LED, four HDMI inputs), and owner discussion leans clearly
        positive. Its known weaknesses — glare in bright rooms and so-so sports motion — are
        common to this entire price tier. The <strong>Vizio Mini LED Quantum</strong> gets brighter
        for less money (Wirecutter's top pick), but independent sources disagree about its picture
        processing, and it requires a Walmart account plus Walmart-exclusive retail. That's a
        values trade-off, not a clear win.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 主推：TCL QM6K -->
    <section class="sec">
      <h2>The safe pick: TCL QM6K</h2>
      <p class="price-line"><span class="lbl mfg">Prices</span> ~$500 for 55" (55QM6K) at Amazon, per Wirecutter's listing, checked Nov 9, 2026. Owner discussion puts street prices between $400–$530 depending on size and sales. Prices change often — verify before buying.</p>

      <h3>What the manufacturer says <span class="lbl mfg">Manufacturer specs</span></h3>
      <p>From TCL's official US product page for the 55QM6K: QD-Mini LED backlight with quantum-dot color, up to LD500 local dimming zones, native 144&nbsp;Hz panel with VRR up to 144&nbsp;Hz, four HDMI inputs (one eARC), Google TV platform, 40&nbsp;W Onkyo 2.1 speaker system, and a one-year limited warranty. HDR support covers HDR10, HDR10+, HLG, and Dolby Vision (including Dolby Vision Gaming and IQ). Sizes run 55" to 98".</p>

      <h3>What independent testing found <span class="lbl lab">Independent testing</span></h3>
      <p>Wirecutter (NYT) named the QM6K its budget-TV runner-up in 2026, praising its fast, accurate local dimming ("consistently inky and immersive shadow tones"), its Google TV platform, and its 144&nbsp;Hz refresh rate for gamers — while noting it is measurably less bright than its top pick (roughly 600 nits versus ~1,000). RTINGS has a full QM6K review and maintains discussion threads from owners on its site.</p>

      <h3>What owners keep saying <span class="lbl user">Buyer feedback</span></h3>
      <p>A Reddit-sentiment analysis of 108 owner mentions (ThreadVerdict, updated Sep 2026) scores the QM6K 8.6/10 with 79% positive: people praise movie and HDR picture quality, big-screen value, and brightness for the price. The same recurring complaints show up across multiple threads: noticeable glare in bright rooms, color washout at side viewing angles, blurry motion during sports, and halo/blooming around subtitles (a side effect of the ~500 dimming zones). A smaller number of owners report quality-control issues — panel banding, flickering when local contrast is enabled, one report of a set dying within six months with slow manufacturer support. These appear to affect a minority of units; how widespread they are is not yet clear from public data.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 分歧选项：Vizio -->
    <section class="sec">
      <h2>The brighter, more divisive option: Vizio Mini LED Quantum</h2>
      <p class="price-line"><span class="lbl mfg">Prices</span> $278 for 55" at Walmart (Wirecutter, checked Nov 9, 2026; a $248 deal price was listed in a syndicated version). Sold exclusively at Walmart and Sam's Club.</p>

      <h3>Where sources agree</h3>
      <p><span class="lbl lab">Independent testing</span> Wirecutter measured around 1,000 nits of peak brightness on the 75-inch model — exceptional for this price — and made it their top budget pick, citing strong color, low input lag, and full HDR format support. <span class="lbl mfg">Manufacturer specs</span>: mini-LED with full-array local dimming, quantum dots, 60&nbsp;Hz native refresh (1080p at 120&nbsp;Hz supported), three HDMI 2.1 inputs, Vizio OS.</p>

      <h3>Where sources conflict</h3>
      <p><span class="lbl lab">Independent testing</span> Other coverage citing RTINGS measurements reports weak black levels, visible blooming, and middling picture processing for this set, ranking it behind pricier rivals — and RTINGS-measured numbers have also been circulated in Chinese-language tech coverage with conflicting port specs (HDMI 2.0 vs 2.1). The two coverage sets disagree, so the Vizio's picture processing stays marked <span class="lbl unk">unverified</span> until RTINGS numbers for this exact US model are checked directly.</p>
      <p><span class="lbl user">Buyer feedback</span> The Walmart-account requirement for smart features is a recurring privacy complaint, and Vizio's smart platform has drawn years of criticism for ads and sluggishness. Some owners bypass it entirely with an external streaming box.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 备选一句带过 -->
    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Also on the shortlist</h2>
      <p><span class="lbl ed">Editorial judgment</span> Based on secondary roundups (smarttvs.org, Sekin's aggregation of RTINGS and Tom's Guide coverage, checked Nov 9, 2026): the <strong>Hisense U6/U65QF</strong> line is repeatedly cited as the brightest HDR alternative under $500, and the <strong>TCL Q6</strong> as the easiest all-rounder if the QM6K is over budget. We have not cross-verified those models against primary sources yet — they're leads, not recommendations.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 谁该跳过这个价位 -->
    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Who should skip this price tier entirely</h2>
      <p><span class="lbl ed">Editorial judgment</span> If your room gets heavy daytime sun, you watch a lot of sports, or you want 4K/120&nbsp;Hz gaming without compromises, the sub-$500 tier will disappoint: every set here trades away sustained brightness, motion handling, or QC polish. Multiple sources point to the $700–$900 mini-LED tier (e.g., Hisense U7, TCL's higher QM tiers) as where those issues fade — that's a consistent pattern across sources, not our measurement.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 待解问题：诚实标注存疑信息的区域 -->
    <section class="sec unk-box">
      <h2>Open questions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>How widespread the QM6K flicker reports are — several owners describe it in RTINGS discussion threads, but no major review lab has covered it, and some owners report no flicker at all.</li>
        <li>Real-time prices. Everything listed above was checked on Nov 9, 2026, from the cited sources; TV prices move weekly, especially into holiday sales.</li>
        <li>Conflicting Vizio port and picture-processing claims between English and Chinese-language coverage — the primary RTINGS numbers for this exact US model weren't reachable during this check.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

